<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mercedes Sosa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Haydée Mercedes Sosa, La Negra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mercedes Sosa was an Argentine singer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-tucuman">San Miguel de Tucumán</a>, rooted in Argentine folk music, who became a leading voice of the nuevo cancionero movement and was called the voice of the voiceless. After a performance at the Cosquín folklore festival in 1965 brought her national attention, she recorded songs by many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> writers, among them Violeta Parra's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gracias-a-la-vida">Gracias a la vida</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> of Jorge Videla she received death threats, was arrested on stage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-plata">La Plata</a> in 1979 along with her audience and was barred from performing, and she went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. She returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> in 1982 and toured widely until her death in 2009.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mercedes-sosa.jpg" alt="Mercedes Sosa" width="330" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Annemarie Heinrich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mercedes_Sosa,_by_Annemarie_Heinrich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was first released in 1969 on the singer <b>Mercedes Sosa</b>'s album Mujeres argentinas.</blockquote><a href="/songs/alfonsina-y-el-mar">Alfonsina y el mar</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mercedes Sosa</b>'s version of 1971, on her album Homenaje a Violeta Parra, made it popular across the Spanish-speaking world.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gracias-a-la-vida">Gracias a la vida</a></li></ul></section>
